WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.110
Alder Works needs three things before a service engagement can start.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:09.150
A schedule that finance can check, an acceptance form with the right account on

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:12.750
it, and a signature the service team can find later.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.550
This is the actual schedule.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:18.151
We generated it in Salesforce from fifty-four fictional service lines.

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:22.471
Let’s work backwards from the file, then follow it forward to acceptance.

00:00:23.108 --> 00:00:26.505
Before we build anything, who actually needs a license?

00:00:26.505 --> 00:00:30.279
Say one person builds templates and four people send agreements.

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:31.789
That’s five staff licenses.

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:36.695
If their customers sign through public links, add one license for the signing

00:00:36.695 --> 00:00:37.828
site’s guest user.

00:00:37.828 --> 00:00:38.960
That makes six.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.244
Each customer gets a private link and can verify their email without buying a

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:44.621
seat.

00:00:44.621 --> 00:00:50.282
Now suppose those customers log in to your portal to sign, or your employees sign

00:00:50.282 --> 00:00:51.037
inside Salesforce.

00:00:51.037 --> 00:00:52.170
That’s authenticated signing.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.811
Ask Portwood for a site license quote.

00:00:54.811 --> 00:00:58.586
It covers the org’s users, including the signing guest user.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:01.623
Here’s the smallest version of the job.

00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:04.749
The template says “Welcome,” followed by Name in braces.

00:01:04.749 --> 00:01:09.611
Select Account as the source, choose Name in the query, and upload the HTML.

00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:14.474
The generated page now says “Welcome, Alder Works.” If you can get that one

00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:18.989
heading right, you’ve proved the connection between the record, the query, and the

00:01:18.989 --> 00:01:19.336
template.

00:01:19.942 --> 00:01:24.671
In the template library, choose Bring an Existing Template when the team already

00:01:24.671 --> 00:01:25.762
maintains a file.

00:01:25.762 --> 00:01:27.945
Word works well for flowing text.

00:01:27.945 --> 00:01:30.492
HTML gives you control over print layout.

00:01:30.492 --> 00:01:33.766
Canvas gives you a page you can arrange visually.

00:01:33.766 --> 00:01:36.312
Excel and PowerPoint keep their native formats.

00:01:36.312 --> 00:01:39.587
Filling a PDF form is a different route again.

00:01:39.587 --> 00:01:42.497
Start with the file the recipient actually needs.

00:01:43.108 --> 00:01:47.621
On Canvas, position the elements, choose fields and preview with a sample record.

00:01:47.621 --> 00:01:52.133
You can link elements, group them into named blocks, and reuse a design.

00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:54.910
For a growing schedule, I prefer a table.

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:59.422
On the Account, select the related Opportunities and the fields for each row.

00:01:59.422 --> 00:02:04.281
Keep a stable sort order so line fourteen stays line fourteen when someone checks

00:02:04.281 --> 00:02:05.322
the printed copy.

00:02:05.942 --> 00:02:09.306
Look at the first and last cells of this row.

00:02:09.306 --> 00:02:11.324
The first opens the Opportunities loop.

00:02:11.324 --> 00:02:12.670
The last closes it.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:16.370
Portwood repeats the whole row, and Row Number counts each entry.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.734
Outside the table, a sum gives us the combined estimate.

00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:22.089
We used optional decimals for the lines.

00:02:22.089 --> 00:02:26.462
One hundred stays one hundred, and one hundred point one keeps its tenth.

00:02:26.462 --> 00:02:28.817
The total always prints two decimal places.

00:02:29.442 --> 00:02:32.166
Here’s where a short preview can fool you.

00:02:32.166 --> 00:02:37.613
On page two, the column headings need to repeat, and a row near the bottom needs

00:02:37.613 --> 00:02:38.635
to stay readable.

00:02:38.635 --> 00:02:42.721
The stylesheet uses a real table header and the renderer’s table pagination

00:02:42.721 --> 00:02:43.061
setting.

00:02:43.061 --> 00:02:46.126
The footer has a second line for page numbering.

00:02:46.126 --> 00:02:49.190
On the third page, the last line is fifty-four.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:52.936
The total is five thousand four hundred dollars and thirty-five cents.

00:02:53.567 --> 00:02:58.709
For a denser executive report, set widths on the columns and give each cell its

00:02:58.709 --> 00:02:59.395
own padding.

00:02:59.395 --> 00:03:03.851
Use solid fills for section bands and put full-page artwork in the background

00:03:03.851 --> 00:03:04.194
setting.

00:03:04.194 --> 00:03:08.993
A browser can draw a gradient, rounded corners, or an inline vector graphic that

00:03:08.993 --> 00:03:10.707
disappears in the final PDF.

00:03:10.707 --> 00:03:13.793
For a branded shape, use a sized image asset.

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:19.621
For charts, use a prepared image on a supported path, or a CSS bar or pivot table.

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:22.897
The service coordinator owns this handoff clause.

00:03:22.897 --> 00:03:25.180
In Assets, open Terms and Snippets.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.604
HTML source shows the fragment; Preview shows the wording.

00:03:28.604 --> 00:03:31.267
The template holds just the snippet key.

00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:35.833
When we generate the schedule, the clause picks up the account name.

00:03:35.833 --> 00:03:38.497
A later approved revision updates future documents.

00:03:38.497 --> 00:03:42.682
The files already sent to clients keep the wording they had.

00:03:43.317 --> 00:03:47.759
For a logo, copy the image tag and give it a print size.

00:03:47.759 --> 00:03:50.835
Deactivate or reactivate the asset with the same key.

00:03:50.835 --> 00:03:54.935
For a typeface, copy the approved font tag into an inline style.

00:03:54.935 --> 00:03:59.719
A stable font name can travel to another org with the same approved face.

00:03:59.719 --> 00:04:03.820
And when a title needs translated wording, use a Salesforce Custom Label.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.237
It resolves in the language of the person running generation.

00:04:07.858 --> 00:04:13.326
If I ask an assistant for a service review without telling it the data shape, it

00:04:13.326 --> 00:04:16.059
can produce a beautiful field that doesn’t exist.

00:04:16.059 --> 00:04:19.135
The designer’s Copy AI Prompt supplies the selected fields.

00:04:19.135 --> 00:04:23.235
Attach the authoring reference for recipes, font and snippet rules, chart limits,

00:04:23.235 --> 00:04:24.261
and Flow contracts.

00:04:24.261 --> 00:04:29.728
In an org with the Agentforce extension, you can also ask for a revision in the

00:04:29.728 --> 00:04:30.070
designer.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:32.462
Generate a PDF to check the answer.

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:34.768
Here is a real AcroForm.

00:04:34.768 --> 00:04:38.510
On the source PDF the account name is an editable field.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:42.252
Our template maps Name and turns on Flatten PDF on generation.

00:04:42.252 --> 00:04:43.953
This is the sandbox output.

00:04:43.953 --> 00:04:48.035
Alder Works is still visible, and the PDF has zero form fields.

00:04:48.035 --> 00:04:52.458
If a client needs to keep typing in the form, leave flattening off.

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:56.881
An older compressed form may need a fresh upload before it can flatten.

00:04:57.483 --> 00:05:03.728
From a record, the runner can download a file, save it to Files, or do both when

00:05:03.728 --> 00:05:04.095
configured.

00:05:04.095 --> 00:05:08.135
A document button can preview the exact file it then downloads.

00:05:08.135 --> 00:05:11.441
An email button walks through template, preview, and recipients.

00:05:11.441 --> 00:05:16.216
Choose None to clear the suggested record email and enter a recipient manually.

00:05:16.216 --> 00:05:18.053
The subject is plain text.

00:05:18.053 --> 00:05:19.889
Check the recipients before sending.

00:05:20.525 --> 00:05:24.384
For a batch, start with a filter you can inspect.

00:05:24.384 --> 00:05:29.401
Record Type developer name and standard lookup fields such as Owner Email work

00:05:29.401 --> 00:05:30.559
alongside custom relationships.

00:05:30.559 --> 00:05:34.804
Check a sample before choosing individual files or a combined PDF.

00:05:34.804 --> 00:05:37.119
A submitted job has an ID.

00:05:37.119 --> 00:05:42.136
The team checks the completed count, errors, and actual files before treating that

00:05:42.136 --> 00:05:43.294
batch as delivered.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:46.916
We already have a letter and a flattened form.

00:05:46.916 --> 00:05:50.268
Merge PDFs takes the exact Salesforce file versions in order.

00:05:50.268 --> 00:05:51.944
We turned on duplex padding.

00:05:51.944 --> 00:05:57.641
In the actual output, the letter is on page one, the form is on page three, and

00:05:57.641 --> 00:05:59.652
pages two and four are blank.

00:05:59.652 --> 00:06:01.998
The action also returns both file IDs.

00:06:01.998 --> 00:06:06.690
For a larger packet, check the input byte budget before sending it through Flow.

00:06:07.317 --> 00:06:12.451
You can send a template, or upload an existing PDF and place fields on it.

00:06:12.451 --> 00:06:17.244
A signature, initials, a signed date and a date the signer picks are different

00:06:17.244 --> 00:06:17.586
fields.

00:06:17.586 --> 00:06:19.640
Assign them to the right person.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:22.378
The active template picker follows the record’s object.

00:06:22.378 --> 00:06:26.828
For an automated request, build the signer collection with a name, email and

00:06:26.828 --> 00:06:27.513
matching role.

00:06:27.513 --> 00:06:31.963
Save the resulting request ID so you can follow the same agreement throughout.

00:06:32.567 --> 00:06:38.240
When the signer is already signed in, put Portwood Sign in Flow on the screen.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.645
For one person, pass the returned Signer URLs collection.

00:06:41.645 --> 00:06:43.914
The component uses its first link.

00:06:43.914 --> 00:06:47.318
Review the document, complete the fields, then confirm consent.

00:06:47.318 --> 00:06:51.101
The Flow can branch on Signed, Declined, and Request complete.

00:06:51.101 --> 00:06:55.640
One person finishing a parallel request is a different milestone from everybody

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:56.018
finishing.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:59.526
Some journeys call for the signing site itself.

00:06:59.526 --> 00:07:04.560
The example Flow extracts one signer’s URL and passes it to Open a Page.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.952
The handoff screen also offers a link if the browser does not open the page.

00:07:09.952 --> 00:07:12.829
Now imagine the signer simply closes that tab.

00:07:12.829 --> 00:07:14.267
Nothing has been signed.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:18.581
Check signing status reads the saved request again, and keeps the interview

00:07:18.581 --> 00:07:20.379
pending until the outcome changes.

00:07:20.983 --> 00:07:24.021
An approval can come from another process entirely.

00:07:24.021 --> 00:07:27.059
Publish a business event after the approval commits.

00:07:27.059 --> 00:07:30.857
A platform-event-triggered Flow reads the approval key, checks the business

00:07:30.857 --> 00:07:33.136
record, and creates the signature request.

00:07:33.136 --> 00:07:37.693
A unique tracking key lets a repeat delivery find the first request.

00:07:37.693 --> 00:07:42.630
If an interactive screen started the work, give it a receipt to check.

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:44.908
The background subscriber prepares the request.

00:07:44.908 --> 00:07:47.187
Navigation happens in the user’s browser.

00:07:47.817 --> 00:07:52.484
Portwood’s own signature PDF event carries a request ID and can identify the

00:07:52.484 --> 00:07:53.920
signer who caused it.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.511
That matters when the second person signs before the first.

00:07:57.511 --> 00:08:02.537
The event can arrive before the final file exists, and some signing paths finish

00:08:02.537 --> 00:08:03.256
without it.

00:08:03.256 --> 00:08:07.923
For fulfillment, check the saved request for Signed and the protected signed file

00:08:07.923 --> 00:08:08.282
ID.

00:08:08.282 --> 00:08:12.591
Use that request ID to keep your downstream action from running twice.

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:19.400
On the signing page a person can type or draw, unless the org or template requires

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.951
typed signatures and initials.

00:08:20.951 --> 00:08:24.055
The page redraws at higher resolution when zoomed.

00:08:24.055 --> 00:08:29.487
Korean and other non-Latin names and dates are supported in the signed file and

00:08:29.487 --> 00:08:29.875
certificate.

00:08:29.875 --> 00:08:32.979
The certificate can include a clickable verification link.

00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:38.023
An authenticated embedded signer uses the same signing service; a guest or a

00:08:38.023 --> 00:08:40.739
prepared uploaded PDF uses the signing site.

00:08:41.358 --> 00:08:46.736
A sender can resend an eligible open request or revoke the outstanding unsigned

00:08:46.736 --> 00:08:47.150
links.

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:51.287
If you revoke, check email status separately from request status.

00:08:51.287 --> 00:08:54.183
A notice needs the configured sender address.

00:08:54.183 --> 00:08:58.320
Brands and email templates control the communication, including the revoked

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:58.733
notice.

00:08:58.733 --> 00:09:01.215
A Portwood User can send requests.

00:09:01.215 --> 00:09:06.180
For embedded signing, an internal non-admin may also need the Portal Signer

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:07.007
permission set.

00:09:07.608 --> 00:09:13.586
When you switch back to an older template version, check the body, query, title

00:09:13.586 --> 00:09:15.294
and page setup together.

00:09:15.294 --> 00:09:18.283
HTML versions keep their own body files.

00:09:18.283 --> 00:09:22.552
Background workbook generation handles sheets in compressed passes and preserves

00:09:22.552 --> 00:09:22.979
macros.

00:09:22.979 --> 00:09:28.530
To move configuration, start the migration command with a dry run between two

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:29.384
nonproduction orgs.

00:09:29.384 --> 00:09:34.081
Review conflicts and mappings, then approve staged fonts in the destination.

00:09:34.081 --> 00:09:37.496
The command’s environment check belongs to the CLI.

00:09:38.108 --> 00:09:42.782
Finance asked for a workbook, and the account team asked for a slide.

00:09:42.782 --> 00:09:45.298
These came from the same sandbox account.

00:09:45.298 --> 00:09:50.331
On the workbook, both the Account tab and the Handoff tab contain the merged

00:09:50.331 --> 00:09:51.050
account name.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:54.645
On the PowerPoint slide, it appears inside the original layout.

00:09:54.645 --> 00:09:57.521
Use individual files for Office output in bulk.

00:09:57.521 --> 00:10:00.037
A macro-enabled workbook keeps its macro format.

00:10:00.037 --> 00:10:03.992
For a PDF deliverable, start with a template that supports PDF.

00:10:04.608 --> 00:10:08.465
If a button you saw here is missing, start with two questions.

00:10:08.465 --> 00:10:12.322
Which version of Portwood is installed, and has the org been upgraded?

00:10:12.322 --> 00:10:15.858
Your Salesforce administrator can find the answer in Setup, under Installed

00:10:15.858 --> 00:10:16.179
Packages.

00:10:16.179 --> 00:10:21.000
If you are current, try the same record as the person who needs the button.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:25.178
Check their permission set, the record page, and whether the template is active.

00:10:25.178 --> 00:10:29.678
Those details give support a much better starting point than a screenshot of an

00:10:29.678 --> 00:10:30.321
empty list.

00:10:30.942 --> 00:10:32.514
Imagine you are the buyer.

00:10:32.514 --> 00:10:36.915
You have four pages in front of you and one question about the Harbor

00:10:36.915 --> 00:10:37.229
configuration.

00:10:37.229 --> 00:10:41.002
Click Harbor on the cover and you land on the right bundle.

00:10:41.002 --> 00:10:42.889
Back to contents takes you home.

00:10:42.889 --> 00:10:45.089
Now add forty-two lines to the quote.

00:10:45.089 --> 00:10:49.491
The commercial terms move from page four to page seven, and the contents number

00:10:49.491 --> 00:10:50.434
moves with them.

00:10:50.434 --> 00:10:54.521
The draft stamp follows every page until the quote is ready to issue.

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:57.985
Here are two field stations on the same order.

00:10:57.985 --> 00:11:02.395
Read down the Atlas table: the enclosure and accessories add up to six thousand

00:11:02.395 --> 00:11:03.655
five hundred forty dollars.

00:11:03.655 --> 00:11:05.230
Harbor has its own components.

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:07.750
Its optional inspection stays outside the committed amount.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:12.160
Up front, the buyer sees ten thousand six hundred seventy dollars once and one

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:13.420
hundred twenty a month.

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:16.256
That is the same deal the sales team priced.

00:11:16.256 --> 00:11:20.351
The document gives each number a place where the buyer can check it.

00:11:20.983 --> 00:11:25.715
Would you walk into this renewal meeting ready to ask for a signature?

00:11:25.715 --> 00:11:29.719
At seventy-two percent adoption, the brief recommends booking an adoption review.

00:11:29.719 --> 00:11:33.723
The team bars make it easy to see where to start.

00:11:33.723 --> 00:11:38.091
Give the same design eighty-four percent adoption and the headline changes to

00:11:38.091 --> 00:11:39.183
preparing renewal terms.

00:11:39.183 --> 00:11:42.822
Take the reading away and it asks for usage data.

00:11:42.822 --> 00:11:46.826
When you brief an AI assistant, spell out that third case.

00:11:46.826 --> 00:11:50.466
An unknown reading and a zero call for different conversations.

00:11:51.067 --> 00:11:55.624
At the equipment cabinet, a technician needs the right unit, the right checks, and

00:11:55.624 --> 00:11:56.600
somewhere to write.

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:59.855
Each card has its own checklist and a QR label.

00:11:59.855 --> 00:12:03.761
Scan the pump label and you get the pump’s identifier, even offline.

00:12:03.761 --> 00:12:07.667
The identifier is printed underneath for a person who cannot scan it.

00:12:07.667 --> 00:12:10.271
The tear-off receipt goes back to the office.

00:12:10.271 --> 00:12:14.177
Try the sheet on the printer your team takes into the field.

00:12:14.817 --> 00:12:17.012
Back in Salesforce, I choose Prepare agreement.

00:12:17.012 --> 00:12:20.776
The screen sends the event, and the background Flow prepares my agreement.

00:12:20.776 --> 00:12:25.795
When I open the signing page, this sandbox asks me to verify the email on my

00:12:25.795 --> 00:12:26.423
Salesforce user.

00:12:26.423 --> 00:12:29.559
Let’s say I close that tab to look something up.

00:12:29.559 --> 00:12:32.069
Back in the Flow, I check the status.

00:12:32.069 --> 00:12:33.637
It still needs my signature.

00:12:33.637 --> 00:12:36.460
I select Reopen my signing page and Check again.

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:37.401
There’s my agreement.

00:12:37.401 --> 00:12:39.911
I can pick up where I left off.

00:12:40.525 --> 00:12:45.023
We started with one field and finished with something a service team can use.

00:12:45.023 --> 00:12:49.842
The schedule adds up, the form keeps the account name, and the workflow has a

00:12:49.842 --> 00:12:52.091
place to check for the signed agreement.

00:12:52.091 --> 00:12:56.588
The guide includes the HTML, footer, example Flow, and the PDFs you’ve seen here.

00:12:56.588 --> 00:13:00.765
Bring one of your own documents, one ordinary record and one difficult record.

00:13:00.765 --> 00:13:03.656
Those three things make a very good next demo.
